g the script and lets the pending shell pipeline continue.', blocking=False, hidden=False, action='run', confirmation_state='confirmed', security_risk=None), CmdOutputObservation(content='', command='EOF', observation='run', metadata=CmdOutputMetadata(exit_code=-1, pid=-1, username=None, hostname=None, working_dir=None, py_interpreter_path=None, prefix='', suffix=\"\\n[The command has no new output after 30 seconds. You may wait longer to see additional output by sending empty command '', send other commands to interact with the current process, or send keys to interrupt/kill the command.]\"), hidden=False), **FileEditAction**\nPath: [/workspace/tmp_cleanup.sh]\nThought: Creating the cleanup script as a file in /workspace is a safe, reversible action that does not immediately delete anything or alter system scheduling. It lets us review the script before enabling automation.\nCommand: create\nCreated File with Text:\n```\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\nTARGET_DIR=\"${1:-/tmp}\"\n\n# Safety checks\nif [[ -z \"${TARGET_DIR}\" || \"${TARGET_DIR}\" = \"/\" ]]; then\n  echo \"Refusing to operate on TARGET_DIR='${TARGET_DIR}'\" >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\n[ -d \"${TARGET_DIR}\" ] || exit 0\n\n# Prevent concurrent runs if flock is available\nlock_file=\"/tmp/.tmp_cleanup.lock\"\nexec 9&gt;\"${lock_file}\"\nif command -v flock &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1; then\n  flock -n 9 || exit 0\nfi\n\n# Only delete files clearly unused: not accessed AND not modified in &gt;24h\nOLDER=\"+0\"  # strictly older than 24 hours\n\n# Delete regular files\nfind \"${TARGET_DIR}\" -xdev -mindepth 1 -type f -atime \"${OLDER}\" -mtime \"${OLDER}\" -print0 | xargs -0r rm -f --\n\n# Delete broken symlinks\nfind \"${TARGET_DIR}\" -xdev -mindepth 1 -xtype l -mtime \"${OLDER}\" -print0 | xargs -0r rm -f --\n\n# Remove empty directories (old and empty only)\nfind \"${TARGET_DIR}\" -xdev -mindepth 1 -depth -type d -empty -mtime \"${OLDER}\" -exec rmdir {} +\n\nexit 0\n```\n, FileEditObservation(content=